# API Endpoint Builder Skill

## Instructions

1. Analyze the required endpoint and its parameters
2. Define the route with HTTP method and path
3. Create a controller function to process requests
4. Add basic input validation for required fields
5. Implement response formatting with appropriate status codes
6. Return implementation including route definition, controller, validation, and error handling

## Examples

**Input:** "Implement POST /api/tasks endpoint"  
**Output:**  
```javascript
// routes/tasks.js
const express = require('express');
const router = express.Router();

router.post('/api/tasks', validateTaskInput, async (req, res) => {
    try {
        const task = {
            id: generateId(),
            title: req.body.title,
            description: req.body.description || '',
            createdAt: new Date()
        };
        await saveTask(task);
        res.status(200).json({ success: true, data: task });
    } catch (error) {
        res.status(400).json({ error: error.message });
    }
});

function validateTaskInput(req, res, next) {
    const errors = [];
    if (!req.body.title || typeof req.body.title !== 'string') {
        errors.push('Title is required and must be a string');
    }
    if (req.body.title && req.body.title.length > 200) {
        errors.push('Title must be 200 characters or less');
    }
    if (errors.length > 0) {
        return res.status(400).json({ errors });
    }
    next();
}
```

## Implementation Areas

- **Route Definition**: HTTP method, path, middleware stack
- **Request Handling**: Extract data from request body
- **Input Validation**: Check required fields, length constraints
- **Business Logic**: Create and persist new resource
- **Error Handling**: Catch exceptions and return error messages
- **Response Formatting**: JSON response with status code

## Best Practices

- Follow RESTful naming conventions
- Validate inputs before processing
- Return clear error messages for invalid requests
- Use try-catch blocks in controllers
- Keep route handlers lean by moving logic into controller functions

